theory.intro:
To graph a piecewise-defined function, sketch each subfunction only on its own interval. At each boundary, work out the value and mark it with a **filled circle** if included or an **open circle** if excluded. The finished graph gives the domain, range and parity.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-0.8,0) -- (6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.6) -- (0,5.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, <-] (-0.6,0.45) -- (2.5,2.5) node[pos=0.4, above left] {$y=g(x)$};
\draw[thick, ->] (2.5,3.8) -- (5.6,4.7) node[pos=0.6, below right] {$y=h(x)$};
\fill (2.5,2.5) circle (2.5pt);
\draw[fill=white] (2.5,3.8) circle (2.5pt);
\draw (2.5,0.12) -- (2.5,-0.12);
\node[below] at (2.5,-0.12) {$a$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.solution_text:
For $x < 0$, $y = x + 2$ is a line with slope $1$, ending at $(0,2)$ with an open circle.
For $x \ge 0$, $y = 2 - x$ is a line with slope $-1$, starting at $(0,2)$ with a filled dot.
The filled dot fills the open circle, so the two lines meet continuously at $(0,2)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (-3.5,0) -- (3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.5) -- (0,4.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.1) {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {-1,1,2,3,4} {\draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.1,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[below left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[thick, <-] (-3,-1) -- (0,2);
\draw[thick, ->] (0,2) -- (3,-1);
\fill (0,2) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

For $x \le 1$, $y = -x - 1$ is a line ending at $(1,-2)$ with a filled dot.
For $x > 1$, $y = x - 1$ is a line starting at $(1,0)$ with an open circle.
There is a jump discontinuity at $x = 1$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (-3.5,0) -- (3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.5) -- (0,3.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.1) {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} {\draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.1,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[below left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[thick, <-] (-2,1) -- (1,-2);
\fill (1,-2) circle (2.5pt);
\draw[thick, ->] (1,0) -- (3.5,2.5);
\draw[fill=white] (1,0) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

For $x \le 0$, $y = 4 - x^{2}$ is the left half of a parabola with vertex $(0,4)$, ending with a filled dot.
For $x > 0$, $y = 4 + x$ is a line starting at $(0,4)$ with an open circle.
The two branches meet continuously at $(0,4)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (-3.5,0) -- (3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.5) -- (0,6.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.1) {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {1,2,3,4,5,6} {\draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.1,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[below left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[thick, <-, domain=-2.3:0, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {4 - \x*\x});
\draw[thick, ->] (0,4) -- (2,6);
\fill (0,4) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

For $x < 1$, $y = 2x$ is a line through the origin ending at $(1,2)$ with an open circle.
For $x \ge 1$, $y = x^{2} + 1$ starts at $(1,2)$ with a filled dot and curves upward.
The branches meet continuously at $(1,2)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (-2.5,0) -- (3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.5) -- (0,5.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2,3} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.1) {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5} {\draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.1,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[below left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[thick, <-] (-1,-2) -- (1,2);
\draw[thick, ->, domain=1:2, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {\x*\x + 1});
\fill (1,2) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

For $x < -1$, horizontal line $y = -2$ with open circle at $(-1,-2)$.
For $-1 \le x \le 1$, line segment from $(-1,-2)$ (filled) to $(1,2)$ (filled).
For $x > 1$, horizontal line $y = 2$ with open circle at $(1,2)$.
The filled dots cover the open circles, making a continuous graph.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (-3.5,0) -- (3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.5) -- (0,3.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.1) {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} {\draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.1,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[below left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[thick, <-] (-3,-2) -- (-1,-2);
\draw[thick] (-1,-2) -- (1,2);
\draw[thick, ->] (1,2) -- (3,2);
\fill (-1,-2) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (1,2) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.solution_text:
For $x \le 0$, line $y = -x$ ends at $(0,0)$ with a filled dot.
For $x > 0$, parabola $y = x^{2} + 1$ starts at $(0,1)$ with an open circle and curves upward.
There is a jump at $x = 0$.
Range from left branch is $[0, \infty)$; from right branch is $(1, \infty)$.
$\text{Range} = [0, \infty)$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (-3.5,0) -- (3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.5) -- (0,5.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.1) {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {1,2,3,4,5} {\draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.1,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[below left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[thick, <-] (-3,3) -- (0,0);
\fill (0,0) circle (2.5pt);
\draw[thick, ->, domain=0:2, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {\x*\x + 1});
\draw[fill=white] (0,1) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

For $x < -1$, $y = x + 3$ ends at $(-1, 2)$ with an open circle.
For $x \ge -1$, $y = x^{2} + 1$ starts at $(-1, (-1)^{2}+1) = (-1, 2)$ with a filled dot.
The filled dot fills the open circle, forming a continuous join at $(-1,2)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (-3.5,0) -- (3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.5) -- (0,5.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.1) {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {1,2,3,4,5} {\draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.1,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[below left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[thick, <-] (-3,0) -- (-1,2);
\draw[thick, ->, domain=-1:2, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {\x*\x + 1});
\fill (-1,2) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

At $x = 0$, left branch reaches $(0,1)$ (open) and middle starts at $(0,1)$ (filled).
At $x = 2$, middle branch reaches $(2,1)$ (filled) and right starts at $(2,1)$ (open).
The graph is continuous everywhere with a flat bottom on $[0,2]$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (-3.5,0) -- (4.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.5) -- (0,4.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.1) {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {-1,1,2,3,4} {\draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.1,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[below left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[thick, <-] (-2,3) -- (0,1);
\draw[thick] (0,1) -- (2,1);
\draw[thick, ->] (2,1) -- (4,3);
\fill (0,1) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (2,1) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

mastery.solution_text:
For $x > 0$, $-x < 0$ so $f(-x) = -(-x)^{3} = -(-x^{3}) = x^{3}$.
For $x > 0$, $f(x) = x^{3}$.
Thus $f(-x) = f(x)$ for all $x$, proving the function is even.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (-2.5,0) -- (2.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.5) -- (0,4.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.1) {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {1,2,3,4} {\draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.1,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[below left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[thick, <-, domain=-1.5:0, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {-\x*\x*\x});
\draw[thick, ->, domain=0:1.5, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {\x*\x*\x});
\fill (0,0) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Left branch $y = 2 - x^{2}$ on $x < 1$:
$y$-intercept at $(0,2)$.
$x$-intercept at $(-\sqrt{2}, 0)$ (since $-\sqrt{2} < 1$).
Endpoint at $(1,1)$ (open).
Right branch $y = 2x - 1$ on $x \ge 1$:
Starts at $(1,1)$ (filled).
No additional $x$-intercept because $x = 0.5$ is not in $x \ge 1$.
Intercepts are $(0,2)$ and $(-\sqrt{2}, 0)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (-2.5,0) -- (3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.5) -- (0,4.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2,3} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.1) {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4} {\draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.1,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[below left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[thick, <-, domain=-1.8:1, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {2 - \x*\x});
\draw[thick, ->] (1,1) -- (2.5,4);
\fill (1,1) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
